hadiths[5]{hadithnumber,text}:
"20946","Rawh narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Ubayd al-Dayli narrated to us from Udaysah daughter of Uhban ibn Sayfi that she was with her father in his house, and he fell ill then recovered from that illness, and Ali ibn Abi Talib stood in Basrah and came to him in his house until he stood at the door of his chamber and greeted, and the elder returned the greeting to him. Ali said to him: How are you, O Abu Muslim. He said: Well. Ali said: Will you not go out with me to these people and aid me. He said: Yes, if you accept what I give you. Ali said: What is it. The elder said: O slave girl, bring my sword. She brought out to him a sheath and placed it in his lap, and he drew part of it, then raised his head to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: My close friend, peace be upon him, and your cousin entrusted to me that when there is strife among the Muslims I take a sword of wood. This is my sword, if you wish I go out with it with you. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We have no need of you nor of your sword. So he returned from the door of the chamber and did not enter."
"20947","Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us from Abu Amr al-Qasmali from the daughter of Uhban that Ali ibn Abi Talib came to Uhban and said: What prevents you from following me. He said: My close friend and your cousin, meaning the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, enjoined me, he said: There will be trials and division, and when that is, break your sword and take a sword of wood. Trial and division have occurred, and I broke my sword and took a sword of wood. And he ordered his family when he became heavy to shroud him and not dress him in a shirt. He said: So they dressed him in a shirt, and in the morning the shirt was upon the drying rack."
"27741","Haddathana Surayj ibn al-Numan who said: Haddathana Hammad, meaning Ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Kabir ibn al-Hakam al-Ghifari and Abd Allah ibn Ubayd from Udaysa from her father: Ali ibn Abi Talib came and stood at the door and said: Is Abu Muslim here; it was said: Yes; he said: O Abu Muslim, what prevents you from taking your share of this affair and acting lightly in it; he said: What prevents me of that is a covenant covenanted to me by my close friend and your cousin; he covenanted to me that when there is strife, I take a sword of wood, and I have taken it, and it is that hanging."
"27742","Haddathana Muammal who said: Haddathana Hammad, meaning Ibn Salama, who said: Haddathana a shaykh called Abu Amr from a daughter of Uhban ibn Sayfi from her father, and he had companionship, that when Ali came to Basra he sent to him and said: What prevents you from following me; so he said: My close friend and your cousin enjoined upon me, and he said: Indeed there will be division and disagreement, so break your sword and take a sword of wood and sit in your house until an erring hand comes to you or a decreed death; so I did what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me; so if you are able, O Ali, not to be that erring hand, then do so."
"27743","Haddathana Aswad ibn Amir who said: Haddathana Hammad ibn Salama from Abu Amr al-Qasmali from the daughter of Uhban from her father that Ali came to Uhban and said: What prevents you from following me; so he mentioned its meaning."
